00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:18.370
My name is Anthony Heilig and
I'm a technical program manager

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:19.510
IN Google Cloud.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:22.720
And Kubeflow is one of
the partner programs

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:23.890
that I am responsible for.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:27.100
And I'll be presenting
this afternoon's session

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:31.480
along with Vinod and
Nicholas from H2O.ai.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:35.890
So you can think of Kubeflow
as TensorFlow packaged up

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:40.640
into Kubernetes containers to
make it easier for everyone

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.110
to deploy, develop, and manage
their portable distributed

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:49.370
machine learning on Kubernetes.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:52.494
Because what we were
seeing is people

00:00:52.494 --> 00:00:53.910
were doing this
in different ways,

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.730
and everybody had
their own solution,

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:56.750
their own architecture.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:00.980
And everybody had rolled their
own custom bespoke thing.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:04.480
And what we started the
Kubeflow project we said,

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.380
you know what, this
looks a lot like what

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.650
we were seeing with
containers before Kubernetes

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:10.910
was developed back in 2014.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.740
So everybody had their own way
of developing their container

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.290
solution and we wanted to
standardize those APIs.

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:21.110
And Kubernetes has
helped us to do that.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:23.210
And it allows the
developers to really focus

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.520
on the higher value things
and not necessarily worry

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.520
about the infrastructure
and how everything else

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.980
is plumbed together.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:33.550
And so if we already
had that Kubernetes,

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:37.000
why wasn't it easy to just
throw TensorFlow into the mix

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.260
and everything work?

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.830
Well, it turns out that there's
a lot you have to do in order

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:43.570
to really make this
work, especially

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.300
the portable distributed
portion of it.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.520
So you would have to become
an expert in containers,

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.760
and packaging,
Kubernetes service

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.080
endpoints, persistent
volumes, et cetera, et cetera.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.570
And we just wanted to eliminate
the developers' responsibility

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:00.310
with becoming an
expert in all of these

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:03.520
and really get them back to
developing their application.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:05.260
And also, once they
develop it once,

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:06.970
being comfortable
that they could run it

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.910
in several different places,
because the goal of Kubeflow

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:13.880
was, Kubeflow is to run
anywhere Kubernetes runs.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.360
So if we could fix this, we
could really make life easier

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.240
for you.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.880
And so that's why
we built Kubeflow.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:25.600
We wanted to turn having
separate experimentation,

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.070
training, and cloud clusters,
on the right-hand side

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:32.620
of this picture,
and this workflow

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.120
that you see on the
left side, which

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.890
is a traditional machine
learning workflow

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:38.170
where you start a
data ingest, and then

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:39.730
you end up with
logging, and then

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:41.780
the loop feeds back on itself.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:44.200
And then you can
retrain, et cetera.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.010
So what this becomes with
Kubeflow is your architecture

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:52.310
becomes a Kubernetes
installation.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:55.460
This workflow is taken
care of with Kubeflow.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.130
And then you can
do experimentation

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:59.740
on your laptop via mini queue.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:03.040
You can do training in
your on-premises cloud

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.400
or some other small cluster
installation that you may have.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.990
And then you can
push to the cloud.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:11.740
And again, Kubeflow makes
all of this seamless

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:17.500
because it's hiding all of the
nuances of getting everything

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.000
from an infrastructure
point of view

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.760
underneath the covers
that is Kubernetes.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.830
And Kubeflow
maintains the machine

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:26.080
learning aspect, the
distributed nature,

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.130
making sure you can use
the different resources,

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:34.280
whether it be CPUs, GPUs,
or TPUs on Google Cloud.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.190
And so what we wanted to
do was to make it easier

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:40.370
to bring these subject matter
experts to machine learning,

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.700
because this is that
next level of unlocking

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:45.350
that we're trying to do.

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.050
Because once we get these
subject matter experts

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:50.360
with the deep domain
expertise that they

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:52.386
can talk more the language
about the problems

00:03:52.386 --> 00:03:54.260
they're trying to solve,
as opposed to trying

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:56.930
to have to deal with all of the
infrastructure and making sure

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.640
everything is plumbed
together and works correctly.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:02.741
This is the vision that we have.

00:04:02.741 --> 00:04:04.490
And so with this
introduction to Kubeflow,

