00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:10.900
So very quick overview on H2O.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:12.527
We call it H2O Core internally.

00:08:12.527 --> 00:08:13.110
So what is it?

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:14.550
It's essentially
a math platform.

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:16.800
So it's an open source
in-memory engine.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:19.170
What we've done is we've
taken the most common machine

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:20.910
learning algorithms
like, as I mentioned,

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:23.880
the GLMs of the world, Random
Forest, gradient boosted trees,

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:25.980
and we rewrote them
from scratch to be fully

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:27.847
parallelized and distributed.

00:08:27.847 --> 00:08:30.180
So you can essentially spin
up multiple machines as long

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:30.480
as your--

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:32.820
if your data is large, you
just spin up more machines

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:34.490
and we can handle all the data.

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:36.929
And that really works well in
the Kubernetes and Kubeflow

00:08:36.929 --> 00:08:39.731
paradigm too, because
when you are training

00:08:39.731 --> 00:08:41.480
on small data on your
laptop, for example,

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:42.896
then if you want
to burst to cloud

00:08:42.896 --> 00:08:46.800
for a much larger dataset,
it's extremely easy to do that.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:50.800
It is written in Java, but we
do offer a really robust REST

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:52.630
API that allows you
to run it from R,

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:56.730
Python, or H2O Flow,
which our own web UI.

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.010
And of course, it is built
for handling big data.

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:02.162
So people have run
terabytes of data,

00:09:02.162 --> 00:09:04.370
and even gone into like
hundreds of terabytes of data

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:06.112
without an issue.

00:09:06.112 --> 00:09:07.320
You can use all of your data.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:08.653
You don't really need to sample.

00:09:08.653 --> 00:09:10.460
That's the real value
proposition of H2O.

00:09:13.492 --> 00:09:15.200
So what kind of
algorithms do we support?

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:17.817
So this is just a select list
of some other algorithms.

00:09:17.817 --> 00:09:20.150
As you can see, most of the
classic statistical learning

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:24.505
algorithms, tree-based methods,
neural networks, deep learning,

00:09:24.505 --> 00:09:26.670
dimension [INAUDIBLE]
algorithms.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:29.930
And we also support a lot of
enterprise learning techniques,

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:32.870
like class training,
time series,

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:35.750
stuff like ISAX,
virtual embedding

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:38.090
like [INAUDIBLE] and TFIDF.

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:42.860
But in addition, we do a lot of
hyperparameter tuning options

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:45.240
that are given, and in
an easy to use format.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.940
One of the things
we did is we created

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:51.110
very thoughtful defaults for
each of those parameters,

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:52.820
so that even as a
new user, if you

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:53.900
don't know what those
parameters mean,

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:55.108
you don't have to figure out.

00:09:55.108 --> 00:09:57.410
It can still run a good
model, good enough model,

00:09:57.410 --> 00:09:59.390
and then come back and
start hitting them.

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:01.490
In addition, we also
added an automatic machine

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:03.167
learning implementation
so that it

00:10:03.167 --> 00:10:05.000
can run through a whole
bunch of algorithms,

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.500
try a bunch of tuning parameters
for you, all automatically,

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:09.964
and give you a good result.

00:10:09.964 --> 00:10:12.130
So when you think about the
typical machine learning

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:14.280
workflow, going back
to Anthony's slide,

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:17.400
on the left side if you will, so
you have your data integration

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:17.940
happening.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:20.760
Bring data in from
different silos or tables,

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.040
do your joins, if you will.

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:25.140
And once you're done,
you have a machine

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:26.377
learning running data frame.

00:10:26.377 --> 00:10:28.710
At each point, you do feature
engineering model building

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:30.430
and a model requirement.

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:34.700
So H2O3 essentially fits in the
model building and deployment

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:35.200
stage.

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:37.159
So you need to do your
own feature engineering.

00:10:37.159 --> 00:10:39.324
You need to do your data
[INAUDIBLE] transformation,

00:10:39.324 --> 00:10:41.520
but once the data is in
a good enough format,

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:44.772
you can use the H2O algorithms
to run on the whole dataset

00:10:44.772 --> 00:10:45.480
and build models.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:49.705
So from an architecture
perspective,

00:10:49.705 --> 00:10:50.580
this is how it looks.

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:53.176
What happens under
the hood is you have--

00:10:53.176 --> 00:10:55.050
on the left, you've got
all the data sources.

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:58.710
Obviously, we support most
common enterprise data sources.

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:00.570
Pull in data from
wherever you have.

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:04.620
Once the data is in H2O, the
H2O data frame essentially,

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:06.450
it's a distributed
key value store

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:10.050
that distributes the whole
data across all the nodes you

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:11.057
have up and running.

00:11:11.057 --> 00:11:12.890
And this is all abstracted
for the end user.

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:15.120
End user doesn't
need to know how

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:17.130
the distribution is happening.

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:19.160
And then you can
do expert analysis

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:20.709
and feature engineering.

00:11:20.709 --> 00:11:22.500
You can run one of the
different algorithms

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:25.410
we support, go through
hyperparameter tuning,

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:27.090
do the evaluation scoring.

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:29.340
And all of these steps
that you do in H2O

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:31.440
are all fully distributed
for you under the hood.

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:33.660
So we take care of
the work, like doing

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:35.390
the non-blocking
hash tables, doing

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:37.957
the in-memory map reduces,
distributed fork joints.

00:11:37.957 --> 00:11:39.540
All those things are
fully abstracted,

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:41.290
so you as an end user
don't have to worry.

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:42.550
So what do you write--

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:47.080
the code that runs on your
laptop, one minor change

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:48.390
will run on a cluster.

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:49.950
So it's as easy as that.

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:52.710
And then the cool part is after
you've done all the modeling,

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:55.680
you can export the artifacts
as either a plain old Java

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.170
object or a model
object and deploy it

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.300
into a whole bunch of
scoring environments.

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:03.126
This code is highly optimized
for low latency scoring.

00:12:03.126 --> 00:12:05.250
So you're talking about
microsecond and millisecond

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:06.810
level latency.

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:09.270
And it is completely independent
of the training cluster.

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:11.020
So you can shut down
the training cluster,

00:12:11.020 --> 00:12:14.400
take the scoring artifact and
put it to deployment anywhere.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:17.910
So it's a really clean end
to end story for enterprises,

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:20.880
and even for any dataset.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:22.750
So that was H2O
Core open source.

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:25.150
So let's look at
Driverless AI for a second.

00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:27.280
So what is Driverless AI?

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:30.920
So we think of it as an expert
data scientist in a box.

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:33.520
So what we did is we
took all the learnings

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:36.460
we had from or five or
six years of open source

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:39.490
usage from the community
and from our customers,

00:12:39.490 --> 00:12:42.440
and we took the learnings from
that and created the software.

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:44.170
So it's actually
built by the experts.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:46.295
The expert data scientists
have built the software.

00:12:46.295 --> 00:12:48.940
We've taken their best
practices, their heuristics,

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:53.290
their workflows, and codified
that in the software.

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:54.820
I'm sure most
people who are here

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:57.340
have heard about Kaggle, right?

00:12:57.340 --> 00:12:59.917
If not, Kaggle is this
dataset is supported

00:12:59.917 --> 00:13:01.000
by people for [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.340
It's actually owned
by Google as well.

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:06.790
But we essentially hired five
of the top 100 grandmasters

00:13:06.790 --> 00:13:09.167
from Kaggle, and we
took their workflows

00:13:09.167 --> 00:13:10.250
and basically codified it.

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:12.760
So you're essentially getting
grandmaster level machine

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:15.530
learning with this product.

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:16.530
And how do we do that?

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:18.900
So again, let's go back
to this enterprise machine

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:19.870
learning workflow.

00:13:19.870 --> 00:13:22.587
So you have data coming
in from different sources.

00:13:22.587 --> 00:13:23.670
You still need to do that.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:25.080
You need to do the ETL part.

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:27.538
You need to do the data quality
and transformation as well.

00:13:27.538 --> 00:13:29.220
We do some of it,
but not a whole lot.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:32.670
But once the data is in a
single data source, if you will,

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:35.100
a single file or a data
frame, at that point on,

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:37.170
you can let Driverless
take care of everything.

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:39.080
So we do all the feature
engineering for you.

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:40.140
We'll do the
[INAUDIBLE] encoding.

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:41.460
We'll do the target encoding.

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:43.740
We'll do dimensional
data reduction for you.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:46.324
We'll find the right
interaction elements.

00:13:46.324 --> 00:13:48.240
And all of those things
automatically for you,

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:49.980
depending on the type
of the dataset, type

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:50.938
of problem [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:50.938 --> 00:13:53.786
So all you need to do
as a user is specify

00:13:53.786 --> 00:13:55.410
what you're trying
to predict and we'll

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:57.345
take care of that point.

00:13:57.345 --> 00:13:59.470
And then in parallel, we
do model building as well.

00:13:59.470 --> 00:14:01.920
So it's a very intertwined
process-- feature engineering

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:05.220
and model building back
and forth iteratively.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:08.220
And goes through this entire
process to give you a pipeline.

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:11.490
This pipeline will now have all
the feature engineering steps

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:15.540
that it thought were useful
and the final model or ensemble

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:17.460
that it picked to be
the most effective.

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:19.620
And this pipeline is
generated as code for you.

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:21.600
So again, going back to
the original paradigm,

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:24.090
you want deployment
ready code that

00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:25.720
is independent of
the training cluster.

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:27.300
So you can take
the code and deploy

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:30.337
into production on the
edge if you want to.

00:14:30.337 --> 00:14:31.920
And finally, we do
another cool thing,

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:35.920
is we actually try to
interpret the model for you.

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:38.570
So what we're trying to
solve is three things.

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:40.570
There's a lot of things
happening in this slide,

00:14:40.570 --> 00:14:43.390
but essentially, it's
talent, time, and trust.

00:14:43.390 --> 00:14:46.420
We found that those are the
three big reasons, hurdles,

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:49.450
obstacles to
enterprise AI adoption.

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:53.800
So talent is-- there is a
deep lack of expert AI talent.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:55.790
That's no news to anyone.

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:58.270
The data scientists are
a hot commodity to find.

00:14:58.270 --> 00:15:01.080
And they are
extremely [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:02.800
hire and keep as well.

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:05.080
So what do all the enterprises
around the world do?

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:06.910
So that's a big challenge.

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:08.320
The second problem is of time.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:13.222
It takes a lot of time to build
models, train them effectively.

00:15:13.222 --> 00:15:14.680
And even with all
the advances that

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:16.654
have happened in
the hardware side,

00:15:16.654 --> 00:15:18.820
you don't have the software
to take advantage of it.

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:21.880
And finally, even once
you build a good model,

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:24.490
these models often don't go to
production because the business

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:25.980
users don't trust them.

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:29.110
Enterprises, the regulators
are afraid of black box models.

00:15:29.110 --> 00:15:31.637
There is inherent lack
of trust in the air.

00:15:31.637 --> 00:15:33.220
So how do we solve
all these problems?

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:36.390
Basically, we solve that by
using Driverless AI to deliver

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:39.040
additional data scientists that
can solve the talent problem.

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:42.010
It can make your existing
work force more effective.

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:46.210
We solve the time problem by
automating the entire workflow,

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:49.930
and also using the latest GPUs
and hardware to accelerate

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:51.370
the machine learning workflow.

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:53.590
So you can give your
results in a matter of hours

00:15:53.590 --> 00:15:55.870
instead of what would
take weeks or months.

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:57.520
And finally, every
model we build,

00:15:57.520 --> 00:15:59.950
we are able to generate
explanations for you that

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:02.654
are fully interpretable.

00:16:02.654 --> 00:16:04.570
So the question comes
up, how accurate are we?

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:05.694
I mean, automation is cool.

00:16:05.694 --> 00:16:08.940
[INAUDIBLE] is the buzzword
now, but is it really good?

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:12.170
So what we did is we actually
took Driverless AI to task

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:14.420
and we participated
in Kaggle as a bot.

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:16.170
So in some of the
competitions [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:16.170 --> 00:16:18.060
so this one in particular
is pretty cool.

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:21.240
The BNP Paribas,
this was a year old.

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:23.430
Out of the box,
Driverless AI came

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:28.080
number 10 on the leaderboard
out of about 3,000 participants.

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:30.720
So just for context,
so all the people

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:34.650
who ranked higher than
Driverless AI, on average,

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:40.285
had about anywhere from
10 to 25 submissions.

00:16:40.285 --> 00:16:42.660
A couple of people had like
over hundreds of submissions.

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:45.180
And then these people
spent weeks, if not months,

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:46.760
to get a higher score.

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:48.750
And in the matter
of a few hours,

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:50.670
Driverless AI came
ranked number 10.

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:54.180
So it can perform at the
level of an expert Kaggle

00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:54.900
grandmaster.

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:56.584
That's what we're
talking about here.

00:16:56.584 --> 00:16:58.125
And this is not just
one competition.

00:16:58.125 --> 00:17:00.166
We participated in a bunch
of other competitions.

00:17:00.166 --> 00:17:02.410
And it typically
comes in the top five,

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:07.849
and sometimes top one percentile
without any human intervention.

00:17:07.849 --> 00:17:09.849
And just to give you a
sneak preview of what's

00:17:09.849 --> 00:17:11.200
happening under
the hood, so you're

00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:12.400
talking about these features.

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:13.839
These are the features
that get created.

00:17:13.839 --> 00:17:15.460
Some of these
automatically generated

00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:18.130
features, things like text
handling, frequency encoding,

00:17:18.130 --> 00:17:23.170
cross validated target encoding,
and truncated SVD, for example.

00:17:23.170 --> 00:17:27.730
These are all complex features
that, A, you don't know--

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:29.710
if you know how
to use them, it's

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:31.684
painful to actually
code them up in Python

00:17:31.684 --> 00:17:34.100
and then try to run them on
all the different combinations

00:17:34.100 --> 00:17:34.780
if possible.

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:36.980
So it's a lot of time
and effort that's

00:17:36.980 --> 00:17:38.920
required if you
know the expertise.

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:42.100
And we do that all
for you [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:46.440
So one final thing
about deployment.

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:48.550
After the model is built,
as I mentioned earlier,

00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:50.970
you do get a Java artifact.

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:53.760
So you can get the
POJO or MOJO for H2.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:56.334
And for Driverless also,
you can get the MOJO file.

00:17:56.334 --> 00:17:57.750
That's essentially
a zip file that

00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:01.860
contains the binary
representation of the model.

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:03.610
And it also contains
all the feature

00:18:03.610 --> 00:18:04.810
engineering information.

00:18:04.810 --> 00:18:06.460
And it's completely
self-independent.

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:08.626
It's self-sufficient, doesn't
have any dependencies,

00:18:08.626 --> 00:18:10.380
so you can put it
into the edge, you

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:13.680
can run it wherever you have
a Java runtime environment.

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:16.650
And obviously, it's easy to add
other language bindings too,

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:18.870
but that's a really
cool deployment story.

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:21.340
So you can have one of these
models running on your phone,

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:22.275
for example.

00:18:22.275 --> 00:18:24.150
And it'll still give
you low latency scoring.

00:18:24.150 --> 00:18:26.470
It can give you some
millisecond response times.

00:18:26.470 --> 00:18:29.506
So you don't have to call
a REST API or an endpoint

00:18:29.506 --> 00:18:30.380
if you don't have to.

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:34.918
We can embed these
applications in your software.

00:18:34.918 --> 00:18:35.418
Cool.

00:18:35.418 --> 00:18:40.770
With that, I am going to invite
Nicholas over to talk about,

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:43.400
how does H2O fit into
Google Cloud's Platform?

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:51.920
NICHOLAS PNG: Hello, everyone.

00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:54.980
So we're just going to
talk of a couple of things.

00:18:54.980 --> 00:18:57.730
First off is just a
couple of pipelines

00:18:57.730 --> 00:19:05.650
that we've run on GCP slash
played with in GCP as a preempt

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:09.760
to the final one, which
is our demo on Kubeflow.

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:11.260
Obviously, it
being in the title,

00:19:11.260 --> 00:19:14.770
it should come last and be
the most important thing.

00:19:14.770 --> 00:19:17.560
So firstly, we have just
a high level deployment

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:22.030
of the pipeline that we might
suggest if you are already

00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:23.020
utilizing Spark.

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:24.700
So a lot of
companies already use

00:19:24.700 --> 00:19:28.550
Spark as part of their
data engineering platform.

00:19:28.550 --> 00:19:30.760
What this does provide
to you is the ability

00:19:30.760 --> 00:19:33.820
to ingest data directly
through Google Dataproc, which

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:36.580
is Google's offering
of Apache Spark.

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:39.122
And it makes things very
easy because you don't have

00:19:39.122 --> 00:19:40.330
to set up the infrastructure.

00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:43.480
Google sets up and pulls up
the infrastructure for you.

00:19:43.480 --> 00:19:45.400
And then what we
can do is actually

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:49.440
play on top of that using our
offering of Sparkling Water

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:50.830
and H2O.

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:52.890
And you can do
your data munging,

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:54.280
you can do your
data engineering.

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:55.790
And then once you're
done with that,

00:19:55.790 --> 00:19:58.840
you can output the final
file into Driverless AI.

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:01.840
From here, Driverless AI can
do all the wonderful things

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:03.220
that Vinod did mention.

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:06.490
We will allow for automatic
feature engineering,

00:20:06.490 --> 00:20:09.370
automatic hyperparameter
tuning and model building.

00:20:09.370 --> 00:20:14.200
And then you can serve
the final model out

00:20:14.200 --> 00:20:19.770
as a MOJO or a Python scoring
package in, again, GCE.

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:25.780
Now, both Driverless AI and
the MOJO scoring pipelines

00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:29.870
can all be served
on VMs in the GCP,

00:20:29.870 --> 00:20:33.010
so on Google Compute Engine.

00:20:33.010 --> 00:20:35.500
And the nice thing is
that you can actually

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:38.530
have a smaller platform
that doesn't necessarily

00:20:38.530 --> 00:20:42.010
need GPUs to serve MOJOs,
because they don't actually

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:44.230
need the GPUs.

00:20:44.230 --> 00:20:47.020
So then another one, just
another quick pipeline

00:20:47.020 --> 00:20:49.420
that we did have is just
going straight from BigQuery.

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:51.920
And this does highlight a little
bit of the integration work

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:55.090
that we've done as far as data
ingest with Driverless AI.

00:20:55.090 --> 00:20:57.576
Essentially, the data
is still stored in--

00:20:57.576 --> 00:21:01.060
well, in this case, it would
be stored directly in BigQuery.

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:04.900
The other ones, obviously,
are kind of moot in this case.

00:21:04.900 --> 00:21:07.360
But you can do the data
munging directly in BigQuery,

00:21:07.360 --> 00:21:09.400
meaning formulate
a select statement

00:21:09.400 --> 00:21:12.970
or formulate a SQL query
that gets you the data

00:21:12.970 --> 00:21:15.310
that you want, at which
point you can actually

00:21:15.310 --> 00:21:18.430
just copy-paste that same
SQL statement directly

00:21:18.430 --> 00:21:22.690
into the Driverless AI UI
through the data ingest.

00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:25.210
At which point you would be
able to just simply ingest

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:27.520
that data and Driverless
will grab that data

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:29.900
and then be able to run with it.

00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:33.210
Same backend of the pipeline.

00:21:33.210 --> 00:21:36.720
And finally, we're going to
just talk a quick highlight

00:21:36.720 --> 00:21:41.670
before we get to a demo here
of just H2O plus Kubeflow.

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:44.910
So the idea being is that the
one nice thing about Kubernetes

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:48.150
and Kubeflow in general is
that, one, Kubernetes allows you

00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:50.610
to distribute your workload.

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:53.250
So you don't necessarily need
to give over a whole machine

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:56.700
to a program that's
running, or only

00:21:56.700 --> 00:22:00.030
really needs one CPU and
maybe a couple gigs of RAM

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:01.950
to support it.

00:22:01.950 --> 00:22:03.900
Now obviously, a
lot of this stuff,

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:06.210
H2O's Core product
in Driverless AI

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:08.640
would require significantly
higher resources.

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:14.520
But the nice thing is, if
you had a cluster on GKE,

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:18.810
you're able to distribute
the workload for H2O Core.

00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:20.310
And then maybe also
on top of this--

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:22.980
and this is something
I'll show in the example--

00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:27.281
also deploy another pod
that contains Driverless.

00:22:27.281 --> 00:22:29.280
And it's all running on
the same infrastructure.

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:31.620
It's all very easy to do,
because all you need to do

00:22:31.620 --> 00:22:34.500
is just give it two Docker
containers, one for H2O and one

00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:38.610
for Driverless AI,
which we do provide.

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:39.750
Cool.

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:42.190
So at this point, let
me just sign in here.

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:47.670
OK, so we're going to
just go over a quick demo.

00:22:47.670 --> 00:22:50.460
Now, what I have here
is, on the left-hand side

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:53.686
is going to be the GitHub repo.

00:22:53.686 --> 00:22:54.810
So this is actually public.

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:59.340
It's H2O AI slash H2O Kubeflow.

00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:01.611
So if you take a look up here.

00:23:01.611 --> 00:23:04.110
This is actually going to be
merged into the actual Kubeflow

00:23:04.110 --> 00:23:05.790
repo eventually.

00:23:05.790 --> 00:23:09.249
I think at the moment we
have an open issue for it.

00:23:09.249 --> 00:23:11.790
And then I just want to show
you my infrastructure right now.

00:23:11.790 --> 00:23:13.950
We have a quick,
small demo cluster.

00:23:13.950 --> 00:23:18.590
So it's not tiny, but
it's not enormous either.

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:22.110
But basically, it comes down
to we have about 32 CPUs

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:24.510
plus some x number of RAM.

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:30.090
And it's a GKE cluster, so
it's running on Google GCP.

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:32.730
Also another quick look,
but basically, the two VMs,

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:35.300
that's all that is there.

00:23:35.300 --> 00:23:37.700
So on the right-hand
side, we have a terminal.

00:23:37.700 --> 00:23:39.390
Now, Kubeflow
basically allows you

00:23:39.390 --> 00:23:42.420
to use something called
Ksonnet as a package that

00:23:42.420 --> 00:23:43.830
allows for templating.

00:23:43.830 --> 00:23:46.740
And this is one of the really
great things of Kubeflow

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:50.580
in my opinion, as an
addition to Kubernetes,

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:53.580
is Kubernetes itself, when
you want to make a deployment,

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:55.950
you have to create a YAML
file or a manifest file

00:23:55.950 --> 00:24:01.410
and say explicitly that this
is the runtime environment

00:24:01.410 --> 00:24:03.810
that my container needs to have.

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:07.050
And now if I go part
two, if, for example,

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:09.525
my original container
was a testing container

00:24:09.525 --> 00:24:14.980
and only needed four CPUs and a
bit of RAM to go along with it,

00:24:14.980 --> 00:24:18.490
then the next one needed
to be much more powerful,

00:24:18.490 --> 00:24:21.429
then you would have to
actually change the YAML file.

00:24:21.429 --> 00:24:23.220
You'd have to actually
change the manifest,

00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:24.912
request more resources.

00:24:24.912 --> 00:24:26.370
And that can be a
pain in the butt,

00:24:26.370 --> 00:24:29.742
especially if you are not
in an environment where

00:24:29.742 --> 00:24:31.200
you can easily
change these things,

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:34.410
or if you're trying to do
it automatically, right?

00:24:34.410 --> 00:24:38.820
The cool thing with Kubeflow
is that if you do ks init--

00:24:38.820 --> 00:24:42.060
and so this is
actually the directory

00:24:42.060 --> 00:24:43.950
that has already been init-ed.

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:47.410
But I'll actually go back just
to show you the idea behind it.

00:24:47.410 --> 00:24:51.600
So if I do ks init and then
demo demo, what that will do

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:54.610
is create--

00:24:54.610 --> 00:24:58.674
yeah, sorry, ks init demo demo--

00:24:58.674 --> 00:25:00.090
what that's doing
is it's actually

00:25:00.090 --> 00:25:04.440
creating a folder,
organizing kubectl to point

00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:08.190
to that specific folder.

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:10.590
And basically, setting up
the runtime environment

00:25:10.590 --> 00:25:17.100
under which you can actually
deploy Kubeflow, as well

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:20.640
as deploy anything
else within that area.

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:23.040
So now we'll actually go
back to Kubeflow demo here.

00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:32.230
Sorry.

00:25:32.230 --> 00:25:34.780
And so what you'll
see here is basically

00:25:34.780 --> 00:25:37.370
what Ksonnet has created.

00:25:37.370 --> 00:25:38.750
So you'll see some environments.

00:25:38.750 --> 00:25:40.040
You'll see some components.

00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:41.560
And you'll see an app.yaml.

00:25:41.560 --> 00:25:45.070
So if we look into components
here, what that's showing

00:25:45.070 --> 00:25:48.190
you is some of the components
that I've already added.

00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:50.140
Ksonnet allows you
to add a registry.

00:25:50.140 --> 00:25:53.350
Now, a registry
actually adds a list

00:25:53.350 --> 00:25:55.150
of packages that
you can install,

00:25:55.150 --> 00:25:57.385
after which you install
specific packages.

00:25:57.385 --> 00:26:01.907
So if I do ks pkg list, you'll
actually see that I've in--

00:26:01.907 --> 00:26:03.490
it'll actually show
you-- the asterisk

00:26:03.490 --> 00:26:04.781
shows that it's installed here.

00:26:04.781 --> 00:26:07.090
So I've installed
our H2O package.

00:26:07.090 --> 00:26:09.280
I've also installed
Driverless AI.

00:26:09.280 --> 00:26:12.310
I've installed the
Kubeflow core packages.

00:26:12.310 --> 00:26:16.540
And these are all running,
so if I do kubectl get pods,

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:19.750
I actually have a couple of them
running already pre-baked so

00:26:19.750 --> 00:26:23.140
that I can actually show you
what's going on the front end

00:26:23.140 --> 00:26:24.580
as well as on the backend.

00:26:24.580 --> 00:26:27.550
So from the point
where we're at here,

00:26:27.550 --> 00:26:31.390
you've installed the packages
using ks pkg install.

00:26:31.390 --> 00:26:33.550
And then you'd actually
just write out--

00:26:33.550 --> 00:26:37.060
so it'd be h2o-kubeflow
in this instance.

00:26:37.060 --> 00:26:39.340
And then I can do /h2o3-scaling.

00:26:43.701 --> 00:26:45.200
So I haven't installed
this package.

00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:47.210
It's basically a
work in progress,

00:26:47.210 --> 00:26:51.200
but gives you the example of how
you would install said package.

00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:56.450
From here, if we
kubectl get pods--

00:26:56.450 --> 00:26:59.630
get sbc-- so this is actually
showing you the endpoints.

00:26:59.630 --> 00:27:04.670
I'm using a load balancer to
expose the port numbers so

00:27:04.670 --> 00:27:09.170
that I can actually use
their APIs in the web browser

00:27:09.170 --> 00:27:12.920
or in a Jupyter notebook
or something of the sort.

00:27:12.920 --> 00:27:17.540
But then the last step after
you've installed the package

00:27:17.540 --> 00:27:25.880
would be ks prototype use
io.ksonnet.pkg.h2o3-static.

00:27:30.660 --> 00:27:33.850
And this is basically
like the first line.

00:27:33.850 --> 00:27:35.610
And you can actually
read through a lot

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:38.057
of these deployment
steps here in the repo.

00:27:38.057 --> 00:27:40.140
So I'm not going to go any
further than giving you

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:42.600
the first step, but
what this is doing

00:27:42.600 --> 00:27:45.604
is that it's actually
creating those components

00:27:45.604 --> 00:27:46.770
that I showed you initially.

00:27:46.770 --> 00:27:50.100
So again, if you ls
into the components,

00:27:50.100 --> 00:27:54.660
you can see that after using
prototype use, what you get

00:27:54.660 --> 00:27:59.700
is this component installed
in the Ksonnet app.

00:27:59.700 --> 00:28:02.100
And if you'll notice
here, you would

00:28:02.100 --> 00:28:04.107
have had to take a
couple parameters

00:28:04.107 --> 00:28:05.440
and pass a couple of parameters.

00:28:05.440 --> 00:28:08.330
So actually, that's going
to be this file right here.

00:28:08.330 --> 00:28:14.900
So if you take a look
at the parameters,

00:28:14.900 --> 00:28:18.246
you can actually see that
it sets some parameters

00:28:18.246 --> 00:28:20.370
for each of the components
that have been deployed.

00:28:25.722 --> 00:28:27.930
So now we're actually going
to just dive a little bit

00:28:27.930 --> 00:28:30.180
deeper into after
you've deployed.

00:28:30.180 --> 00:28:35.370
So sorry, just one last
quick thing. ks apply

00:28:35.370 --> 00:28:37.580
is going to be how
you actually deploy.

00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:40.440
So ks apply the
specific environment

00:28:40.440 --> 00:28:43.330
that you want, and then
also the component.

00:28:43.330 --> 00:28:47.220
So in this case, h2o3-static
would be one of the components

00:28:47.220 --> 00:28:48.750
that I had already created.

00:28:48.750 --> 00:28:51.420
When you do that, what you're
ultimately going to get

00:28:51.420 --> 00:28:54.150
is the package along
with the parameters is

00:28:54.150 --> 00:28:56.160
going to be passed to Kubeflow.

00:28:56.160 --> 00:28:58.830
And that's going to
deploy your packages

00:28:58.830 --> 00:29:01.470
and pull up as many
replicas as were requested,

00:29:01.470 --> 00:29:04.680
as well as pull up the
specific resources allocated

00:29:04.680 --> 00:29:06.990
to each pod.

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:10.800
So now-- we're going to go
quickly first to the H2O

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:12.030
deployment.

00:29:12.030 --> 00:29:13.720
And this is H2O Core.

00:29:13.720 --> 00:29:18.210
So if you go to the external
API in the load balancer,

00:29:18.210 --> 00:29:20.880
and it's just HTTP
and then 54321,

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:23.800
which is the exposed port, you
can see that this is H2O Flow.

00:29:23.800 --> 00:29:26.400
So this is actually what Vinod
was talking about earlier.

00:29:26.400 --> 00:29:29.070
This is our basically
simplified GUI.

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:31.500
So it's very similar to
Jupyter notebook in the sense

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:36.480
that it gives you a
step-through notebook.

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:39.984
And you can do some
more simplistic steps.

00:29:39.984 --> 00:29:41.400
Rather than coding
this in Python,

00:29:41.400 --> 00:29:43.800
you can actually
just go import files.

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:46.170
And I can set a
variable that says

00:29:46.170 --> 00:29:52.680
variable is locA for location
A is equal to some string here.

00:29:52.680 --> 00:29:58.900
And then import the
file, just like this.

00:29:58.900 --> 00:30:01.630
And instead of using
Pythonic language,

00:30:01.630 --> 00:30:08.686
it's very clearly just one step
next, do the next thing, right?

00:30:08.686 --> 00:30:10.560
And I'm going to go
ahead and leave that now.

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:13.995
So now, if we go to
a Jupyter notebook.

00:30:16.822 --> 00:30:17.530
Sorry about that.

00:30:24.985 --> 00:30:27.450
Let's open up the demo.

00:30:27.450 --> 00:30:29.970
So I have-- just basically,
this is just a quick notebook

00:30:29.970 --> 00:30:31.770
showing you what would happen.

00:30:31.770 --> 00:30:32.610
You would import.

00:30:32.610 --> 00:30:34.680
And this would be from
your local computer.

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:38.520
You can import the H2O packages,
connect to the specific URL

00:30:38.520 --> 00:30:39.690
plus endpoint.

00:30:39.690 --> 00:30:42.750
And it will actually
show you the resources.

00:30:42.750 --> 00:30:44.550
So we have a cluster
of three nodes.

00:30:44.550 --> 00:30:47.520
That's because that's the
deployment package that I made.

00:30:47.520 --> 00:30:50.290
Go ahead and import some data.

00:30:50.290 --> 00:30:51.540
Go ahead and set up some data.

00:30:51.540 --> 00:30:54.000
And this is running
through a demo

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:55.660
that's on our documentation.

00:30:55.660 --> 00:30:57.912
So if you do look
at docs.h2o.ai,

00:30:57.912 --> 00:30:59.370
you can actually
see the same demo,

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:01.610
or something fairly similar.

00:31:01.610 --> 00:31:03.360
Ultimately, what this
is coming down to is

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:06.150
we're running H2O's
AutoML package.

00:31:06.150 --> 00:31:09.120
The AutoML meaning that it's
going to do an intense grid

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:13.560
search across several different
algorithms that we have

00:31:13.560 --> 00:31:15.250
and try and find the best one.

00:31:15.250 --> 00:31:16.800
And you can track progress here.

00:31:16.800 --> 00:31:19.310
Basically, all I've done
is set a single parameter

00:31:19.310 --> 00:31:22.020
here saying that don't
run more than 120 seconds.

00:31:22.020 --> 00:31:23.640
But basically,
take any algorithms

00:31:23.640 --> 00:31:25.710
that you want, try
as much as you can,

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:27.910
and give me the best
possible result.

00:31:27.910 --> 00:31:29.520
So while it's
running, I'm actually

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:33.180
going to show you the other
product that's running,

00:31:33.180 --> 00:31:34.680
which is Driverless AI.

00:31:34.680 --> 00:31:38.610
So Driverless AI, it's
exposed at this IP right here,

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:41.100
and just the port number 12345.

00:31:41.100 --> 00:31:45.070
So if I go 12345, you'll see
Driverless AI is running.

00:31:45.070 --> 00:31:48.600
And I've actually loaded a
couple of datasets already.

00:31:48.600 --> 00:31:50.220
In case you're not
familiar with this,

00:31:50.220 --> 00:31:53.640
the first step would be going
through and adding a dataset.

00:31:53.640 --> 00:31:57.840
We do have direct integration
to Google BigQuery, as well as

00:31:57.840 --> 00:31:59.130
Google Cloud Storage.

00:31:59.130 --> 00:32:01.710
So if I were wanting to
go to Google BigQuery,

00:32:01.710 --> 00:32:07.080
again, you can go and type
in the dataset ID right here.

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:10.869
You can type in the
GCS bucket right here.

00:32:10.869 --> 00:32:12.535
And then you can do
some sort of select.

00:32:12.535 --> 00:32:17.550
So SELECT * FROM DATASET.

00:32:20.170 --> 00:32:21.640
Just in the interest
of time, I'm

00:32:21.640 --> 00:32:23.306
actually just going
to close that window

00:32:23.306 --> 00:32:25.480
and show you from
what we have already.

00:32:25.480 --> 00:32:29.140
So what I have here is
one time series datasets.

00:32:29.140 --> 00:32:32.680
So this is a Walmart dataset
that was from Kaggle.

00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:39.560
Basically, it's predicting the
weekly sales of a Walmart store

00:32:39.560 --> 00:32:41.520
in series of time.

00:32:41.520 --> 00:32:43.270
So the first thing
that Driverless AI does

00:32:43.270 --> 00:32:44.728
is it gives you a
couple of options

00:32:44.728 --> 00:32:46.160
straight from the Dataset tab.

00:32:46.160 --> 00:32:47.320
First, you can describe it.

00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:49.060
So basically, what this
is doing is giving you

00:32:49.060 --> 00:32:50.210
some statistical inference.

00:32:50.210 --> 00:32:53.020
It's telling you what
type of values they are,

00:32:53.020 --> 00:32:55.300
how many values there
are, and if there

00:32:55.300 --> 00:32:59.260
are any missing, and
mean, min, max, just

00:32:59.260 --> 00:33:01.210
some very basic statistics.

00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:03.370
A lot of you would see
something fairly similar

00:33:03.370 --> 00:33:07.750
if you went to pandas
and did pandas.describe.

00:33:07.750 --> 00:33:10.000
The next thing that we do
have is visualizations.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:12.310
So this is a package
that we call AutoVis.

00:33:12.310 --> 00:33:14.740
AutoVis basically gives
you the opportunity

00:33:14.740 --> 00:33:21.220
to create visualization
plots automatically.

00:33:21.220 --> 00:33:23.710
So Driverless does this
automatically for you.

00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:27.490
And what it's going to
show is a lot of the plots

00:33:27.490 --> 00:33:30.310
that, as a data scientist,
you probably want to know.

00:33:30.310 --> 00:33:32.440
Outlier plots are
very important.

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:35.140
Seeing if that maybe,
for some reason,

00:33:35.140 --> 00:33:37.900
the whole distribution is
here, but for some reason

00:33:37.900 --> 00:33:40.780
there's some data
points that are kind

00:33:40.780 --> 00:33:42.350
of lying way out on the side.

00:33:42.350 --> 00:33:44.980
And so this would be on
a specific column here.

00:33:44.980 --> 00:33:48.820
And if you scroll through, you
can see each different column.

00:33:51.760 --> 00:33:54.040
Same goes for-- you
can get radar plots.

00:33:54.040 --> 00:33:56.920
You can get histograms
or skewed histograms.

00:33:56.920 --> 00:33:59.170
Correlation graphs
are also really cool.

00:33:59.170 --> 00:34:03.026
And you can see the correlation
between store or is holiday,

00:34:03.026 --> 00:34:04.900
everything seems to be
correlated is holiday.

00:34:04.900 --> 00:34:09.100
And that makes a lot of sense
considering this is retail.

00:34:09.100 --> 00:34:12.010
And then after this, you
can create an experiment.

00:34:12.010 --> 00:34:16.900
So the first steps, we're all
like, oh, is the data good?

00:34:16.900 --> 00:34:19.449
Did I import the right dataset?

00:34:19.449 --> 00:34:21.880
Do I feel comfortable with
running experiment on this?

00:34:21.880 --> 00:34:24.230
Once I go there, now
I can go to predict.

00:34:24.230 --> 00:34:25.550
I'm ready to do a prediction.

00:34:25.550 --> 00:34:28.780
And so at this point,
you can run a prediction.

00:34:28.780 --> 00:34:31.639
And that means adding a test
dataset if you so choose.

00:34:31.639 --> 00:34:34.420
If you do not add a
test dataset, it's fine.

00:34:34.420 --> 00:34:37.239
Driverless AI will
notice and automatically

00:34:37.239 --> 00:34:40.360
use cross-validation
folds to test and train.

00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:45.070
So it will do testing
and validation

00:34:45.070 --> 00:34:50.050
on the out-of-fold data
so that you don't ever

00:34:50.050 --> 00:34:54.630
have to worry about, well,
about cheating, essentially.

00:34:54.630 --> 00:34:57.760
So time column-- this is
a time series dataset.

00:34:57.760 --> 00:35:00.890
It's one of those things where
we do need to address time.

00:35:00.890 --> 00:35:02.530
And we don't want
to look at September

00:35:02.530 --> 00:35:05.020
to predict August sales, right?

00:35:05.020 --> 00:35:08.751
So you can always set
Auto for time column.

00:35:08.751 --> 00:35:10.750
Otherwise, if you know
the specific time column,

00:35:10.750 --> 00:35:12.416
you can actually go
in and say, OK, well

00:35:12.416 --> 00:35:14.621
that's actually just the date.

00:35:14.621 --> 00:35:16.120
And then from here,
you can actually

00:35:16.120 --> 00:35:17.495
select how many
weeks do you want

00:35:17.495 --> 00:35:20.080
to predict forward,
how many weeks after do

00:35:20.080 --> 00:35:21.740
you want to predict.

00:35:21.740 --> 00:35:24.370
And always, Auto is optional.

00:35:24.370 --> 00:35:27.790
Otherwise, you can go in and
select it more specifically.

00:35:27.790 --> 00:35:29.890
Then finally, as we
said, a target column.

00:35:29.890 --> 00:35:32.060
So this, in this case,
would be weekly sales.

00:35:32.060 --> 00:35:34.430
And from here, you
get the final step,

00:35:34.430 --> 00:35:37.820
which is you select your score.

00:35:37.820 --> 00:35:41.200
So RMSE, RMSLE,
RMSPE, MAE, these

00:35:41.200 --> 00:35:43.840
are all scores for regression.

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:46.790
And the reason is because
you're predicting a revenue.

00:35:46.790 --> 00:35:48.517
So this would be a
regression problem.

00:35:48.517 --> 00:35:49.600
And you have three knobs--

00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:51.820
Accuracy, Time, and
Interpretability.

00:35:51.820 --> 00:35:53.710
Interpretability is just
how interpretable do

00:35:53.710 --> 00:35:57.020
you want on a scale of 1 to 10.

00:35:57.020 --> 00:35:59.680
0 interpretability,
or 1, would mean

00:35:59.680 --> 00:36:03.160
that you don't mind having a
very complex model, versus 10

00:36:03.160 --> 00:36:05.420
is saying make it as
simple as possible.

00:36:05.420 --> 00:36:08.980
Make it so that the feature
transformations are all very

00:36:08.980 --> 00:36:11.320
clear and easy to understand.

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:16.870
Time-- less time means take a
shorter amount of time to run,

00:36:16.870 --> 00:36:20.050
or more time, meaning take
longer to run, I don't mind.

00:36:20.050 --> 00:36:21.430
Run as long as you need.

00:36:21.430 --> 00:36:23.900
Accuracy-- how high do
I prioritize accuracy?

00:36:23.900 --> 00:36:25.720
So is accuracy more important?

00:36:25.720 --> 00:36:28.780
Can I have a more complex
model, things like that.

00:36:28.780 --> 00:36:31.850
At which point, then you
can click Launch Experiment.

00:36:31.850 --> 00:36:34.990
So once you launch, it will
give you this kind of UI.

00:36:34.990 --> 00:36:37.790
From here, you can
watch as we progress

00:36:37.790 --> 00:36:39.790
through the [INAUDIBLE]
and see more iterations.

00:36:39.790 --> 00:36:42.820
As variables change, you'll
see variable importance here.

00:36:42.820 --> 00:36:47.020
And you can check on CPU usage,
an actual versus predicted

00:36:47.020 --> 00:36:49.840
graph, as well as
some just logging

00:36:49.840 --> 00:36:52.420
and trace values up front.

00:36:52.420 --> 00:36:55.307
I actually have already
completed data, or passed,

00:36:55.307 --> 00:36:56.890
so I'm actually just
going to show you

00:36:56.890 --> 00:36:59.330
that right here so that we
can talk about it a bit.

00:36:59.330 --> 00:37:02.030
But what it comes down to
is you can see now the graph

00:37:02.030 --> 00:37:03.670
has filled itself in.

00:37:03.670 --> 00:37:05.200
You can actually
take a look here.

00:37:05.200 --> 00:37:07.116
This is kind of cool,
because this is actually

00:37:07.116 --> 00:37:11.350
showing that the only data
point that was actually

00:37:11.350 --> 00:37:14.350
kept that is from
the original dataset

00:37:14.350 --> 00:37:16.060
is Department, of the top five.

00:37:16.060 --> 00:37:19.870
So you can see that some of
them are target lag values.

00:37:19.870 --> 00:37:21.310
One of them is a
clustered value.

00:37:21.310 --> 00:37:24.370
So this is using a clustering
algorithm of our making,

00:37:24.370 --> 00:37:28.480
so we clustered the values
between Department and Markdown

00:37:28.480 --> 00:37:29.350
number 4.

00:37:29.350 --> 00:37:31.300
And from that, we
actually created

00:37:31.300 --> 00:37:34.600
a new value that
is going to become

00:37:34.600 --> 00:37:37.600
the value in that column.

00:37:37.600 --> 00:37:40.710
And then finally, there's also
another lag value at the top

00:37:40.710 --> 00:37:41.210
here.

00:37:41.210 --> 00:37:43.360
So the other thing you
can take a look at here

00:37:43.360 --> 00:37:44.110
is the iteration.

00:37:44.110 --> 00:37:46.420
So over time, it started
out with a couple models.

00:37:46.420 --> 00:37:47.920
And you can see
there's some outlier

00:37:47.920 --> 00:37:51.350
models which were really,
really not high performant.

00:37:51.350 --> 00:37:55.600
Obviously, RMSE meaning the
lower RMSE the better, right?

00:37:55.600 --> 00:37:59.770
So we started out at
around 2,600 RMSE.

00:37:59.770 --> 00:38:02.590
And then finally, the final
model they were predicting

00:38:02.590 --> 00:38:04.130
is a little bit less than that.

00:38:04.130 --> 00:38:06.190
So over time,
basically-- and this

00:38:06.190 --> 00:38:09.940
is if we had given it more time,
if we had made it a little bit

00:38:09.940 --> 00:38:12.520
less interpretable, if we had
higher prioritized accuracy,

00:38:12.520 --> 00:38:14.770
it'll progress a
little bit more.

00:38:14.770 --> 00:38:18.760
But for these
settings, this is what

00:38:18.760 --> 00:38:21.280
you would expect is that over
time, we'd progress and find

00:38:21.280 --> 00:38:24.340
a certain point where it
would stop, because it said,

00:38:24.340 --> 00:38:25.490
OK, this was good enough.

00:38:25.490 --> 00:38:28.970
This is kind of what
you were expecting.

00:38:28.970 --> 00:38:32.070
And I'm just going to
quickly talk about this.

00:38:32.070 --> 00:38:35.447
We didn't run a pre-baked
MLI, but this is machine

00:38:35.447 --> 00:38:36.530
learning interpretability.

00:38:36.530 --> 00:38:38.655
Essentially, what it comes
with is three packages--

00:38:38.655 --> 00:38:40.550
or three surrogate models--

00:38:40.550 --> 00:38:44.700
a random forest, a singular
decision tree, and K-LIME.

00:38:44.700 --> 00:38:48.170
So K-LIME is essentially LIME,
but K number of LIME models.

00:38:48.170 --> 00:38:50.000
What that does is
it creates a series

00:38:50.000 --> 00:38:53.390
of linear models,
essentially, that approximate

00:38:53.390 --> 00:38:56.220
what the actual model is doing.

00:38:56.220 --> 00:38:58.580
So it's basically trying
to give you linear betas.

00:38:58.580 --> 00:39:00.330
The same idea as like
a linear regression,

00:39:00.330 --> 00:39:02.750
you have a beta
on each point that

00:39:02.750 --> 00:39:09.080
will explain how a
change in that data point

00:39:09.080 --> 00:39:11.880
would affect the prediction.

00:39:11.880 --> 00:39:12.651
So that's K-LIME.

00:39:12.651 --> 00:39:14.150
The next one is
random forest, which

00:39:14.150 --> 00:39:17.390
will give you local
plots, which are basically

00:39:17.390 --> 00:39:20.816
feature importance, but
positive and negative.

00:39:20.816 --> 00:39:22.190
There's also a
feature importance

00:39:22.190 --> 00:39:23.030
which is just positive.

00:39:23.030 --> 00:39:24.780
And then finally,
there's a decision tree

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:30.630
which allows you to trace the
path of a singular decision

00:39:30.630 --> 00:39:31.130
tree.

00:39:31.130 --> 00:39:32.490
Very straightforward.

00:39:32.490 --> 00:39:36.230
If I saw it at this specific
column, why I made a decision.

00:39:36.230 --> 00:39:39.280
